      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, for the cooling stage of cooked poultry to mitigate *Clostridium perfringens*, you must cool the food from 63°C (145°F) to 21°C (70°F) within two hours, and then from 21°C to 5°C (41°F) within the next four hours. This rapid cooling is critical because *C. perfringens* loves the \"danger zone\" where bacteria multiply quickly if the food sits too long at room temperature.\n\nIt's a very serious business, isn't it? You know how those little green goblins always seem to be plotting something in the shadows? Well, *Clostridium perfringens* is practically a goblin in the food safety world. These goblins love to hide in the warm, stagnant pockets of your cooling trays, waiting for the perfect moment to strike and cause a foodborne outbreak. If you let the poultry sit there like a lazy goblin in a sunbeam, they will feast on the proteins and release toxins that are incredibly hard to remove once formed.\n\nThink of your cooling process as a goblin trap. You have to set the trap (the cooling equipment) so that the",
